00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:08.540
you know you know speaking for myself i was&nbsp;
really pleased to be invited to give this talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.980
and to have the chance to speak&nbsp;
directly to early career scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.300
who have so much promise to do good&nbsp;
work and to make positive change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.900
i don't however come to you as an expert&nbsp;
on robots or artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.660
but i do have one or two things to say about&nbsp;
algorithms because although algorithmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:35.580
thinking has gotten faster more efficient and more&nbsp;
intelligent so to speak the use of algorithms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:43.020
the medical workplace is a fairly old technology&nbsp;
still i do think i owe you a bit of an explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:49.100
about when and where i enter this conversation&nbsp;
so that you can better understand what's to come

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.660
so i'm a sociologist who thinks about the&nbsp;
reproduction of inequality and injustice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:04.540
and so i come to the field of bioethics as&nbsp;
less a philosopher than a social scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:12.780
and as a social scientist i focus quite deeply on&nbsp;
everyday workplace interactions i see places like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:19.020
clinics and hospitals as not just places where&nbsp;
patients go to have their conditions treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:26.140
but it's places where people go to work so&nbsp;
i'm less interested in abstract questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:32.620
of what one ought to do and more interested&nbsp;
in ascertaining how workers understand what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:38.780
one might do which is to say i'm interested&nbsp;
in understanding how choice sets get created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:45.580
in the medical workplace because day and night&nbsp;
workers in health care are making decisions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.940
some of these decisions are mundane or routine&nbsp;
and some of them are life or death but all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:56.700
them shape patient outcomes and experience and&nbsp;
i'm particularly interested in understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:04.140
why it seems to be the case that reproducing&nbsp;
inequality is so often the default choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.940
for those decisions because make no&nbsp;
mistake that does seem to be the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:17.820
because health disparities are a real and robust&nbsp;
finding in the u.s context black people have worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.020
health outcomes than the white populations so&nbsp;
you might want to know with a little bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:29.660
specificity about which outcomes exactly i'm&nbsp;
talking about and i can safely say pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:35.900
all of them when i first started studying health&nbsp;
disparities this was one of the most startling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:42.620
facts that i learned because whether you're&nbsp;
talking about chronic diseases like diabetes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:48.140
or looking at a range of different forms&nbsp;
of cancer on almost every measure african&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:54.380
americans have a higher prevalence and a poorer&nbsp;
prognosis and when you see the same trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.380 --> 00:06:00.540
for so many outcomes it starts to seem unlikely&nbsp;
that they're a product of different mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:07.820
so scholars began searching for common mechanisms&nbsp;
right and so in doing so they turned their gaze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:13.340
away from the bodies of individuals and their&nbsp;
habits and upstream to the level of structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:19.500
so scholars began looking at factors that&nbsp;
affect whole communities and populations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:25.820
such as residential segregation environmental&nbsp;
racism and differential access to quality k-12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:30.140
education now most of these&nbsp;
structural causes are produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:37.580
outside the purview of medicine in the medical&nbsp;
workplace except that is for racism itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:44.140
you have scholars like david williams bruce link&nbsp;
and joe phelan who have argued that racism is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:51.580
common fundamental cause of both downstream and&nbsp;
upstream factors and the place where racism is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.100
most visible and impactful in health care&nbsp;
is at the level of the medical encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.860
so we know for example that provider bias is&nbsp;
a real and observable practice such that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:12.460
patient's race is an independent predictor of low&nbsp;
quality care so when a 2005 report published and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:18.300
produced by the institute of medicine they found&nbsp;
for example that race the race of the patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.740
predicted a lower likelihood of receiving&nbsp;
appropriate cardiac care receiving kidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:29.420
dialysis or transplants or receiving the best&nbsp;
treatments for stroke cancer or aids treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:35.580
and even when you hold constant variables like&nbsp;
insurance status income age and severity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:42.700
condition these these relationships remain robust&nbsp;
now the most commonly cited explanation for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:49.020
differences in care is implicit bias so implicit&nbsp;
bias is something that all human beings do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:54.700
right our lightning fast cognition relies&nbsp;
on stereotyped rules of thumb for its speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:02.140
but the shape of many of these rules are not&nbsp;
hardwired but are culturally and socially produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:09.580
sociologist eduardo benia silva has written about&nbsp;
white americans penchant of segregating themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:16.140
from others in social life and housing education&nbsp;
informal socializing and family formation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.980
i might add that this segregation is achieved not&nbsp;
through peaceful preference but generally through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:29.420
illegal or violent forms of exclusion so for&nbsp;
bania silva this segregating behavior has created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:36.780
what he calls a white habitus such that racialized&nbsp;
thinking and its associated biases go unchallenged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:43.020
through intra-racial socializing or meaningful&nbsp;
relationships so since white healthcare workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.940
are no less likely to have been socialized in a&nbsp;
white habitus they come to work with many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:56.460
same implicit biases as the general population i&nbsp;
also don't want to leave out explicit bias because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:01.420
although implicit bias is easier to talk about&nbsp;
and so it often receives the most focus in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:08.140
literature and in the popular works but explicit&nbsp;
racial bias by healthcare providers is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:15.340
a real and observable practice so quantitatively&nbsp;
a 22 survey whose results were published in jama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:20.780
found that 21 of patients report having&nbsp;
experienced an overt act of racial discrimination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:29.740
the qualitative data is even more evocative in&nbsp;
her 2019 book invisible visits scholar tina sax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:35.180
talks about the ways that black middle-class&nbsp;
women navigate the health care system and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:40.620
book is notable because again she's focused on&nbsp;
black middle-class women so class or access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:47.660
health care is not the predominant factor and&nbsp;
yet her respondents described in gruesome graphic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:53.820
detail a lifetime of healthcare encounters that&nbsp;
ranged from the uncomfortable to the traumatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.020 --> 00:10:00.140
but i think it's important to point out that&nbsp;
racial ideology and thought and practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:06.540
is not as some may think just the residual left&nbsp;
over um from science i.e it's just standing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:13.340
for the leftover unscientific beliefs yet this&nbsp;
racial ideology is often implicated within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:20.860
the education and training of health care workers&nbsp;
so sociologist anne warning did a 2012 study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:26.860
examining the way that scientists write about&nbsp;
and teach racial difference and she found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:32.620
the idea that race is a biologically real and&nbsp;
relevant category is alive and well in science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:38.780
and so when providers in training are poised&nbsp;
to believe through that training that race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:44.220
is biologically real then it becomes easier&nbsp;
for theories of racial difference to take hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:52.060
and take hold it has and it's rather famous&nbsp;
now 2016 study by hoffman and colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:57.100
they found that a surprising number of medical&nbsp;
students and residents endorsed what they call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:02.940
false racial beliefs such as that black people&nbsp;
have thicker skin than white folks that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.020
age more slowly than whites and that they're&nbsp;
more impervious to pain moreover the study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:15.020
found that a belief in these false beliefs was&nbsp;
correlated with medical errors and pain assessment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:20.860
and since pain assessment is a diagnostic&nbsp;
criteria for many conditions and illnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.500
the potential impact of these beliefs is probably&nbsp;
much broader than the pain assessment itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:32.060
and these findings refer specifically to medical&nbsp;
students and residents but since medical knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:38.380
is to some extent a shared resource it's likely&nbsp;
that nurses respiratory therapists occupational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:45.340
therapists also believe in at least some of this&nbsp;
racialized knowledge and this is where the promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:51.740
of algorithmic intelligence comes in because to&nbsp;
the extent that racism is a part of the medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:57.340
encounter and is reproduced within it it has&nbsp;
the potential ai has the potential to disrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:02.540
its reproduction primarily by reshaping the&nbsp;
behavior of the provider within the interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:09.900
so explicit bias and diagnosis and treatment will&nbsp;
be much more difficult to perform when there is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:16.300
algorithm alerting them that they're not following&nbsp;
the evidence in terms of care and when that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:22.220
algorithm is not just in a published guideline&nbsp;
but is rather embedded in an automated tool but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:28.300
they will have to affirmatively account for if&nbsp;
they um decide to deviate from that decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:33.500
which is another way of saying the burden of&nbsp;
proof will be higher when one decides against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:40.460
standard of care produced by the algorithm it also&nbsp;
has the potential to reshape implicit bias so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:45.820
know from the psych literature that when you make&nbsp;
people aware of their hidden or implicit biases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:51.660
they're also better able to through conscious&nbsp;
action think their way towards less bias so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:59.500
ai then becomes a part of a set of cognitive new&nbsp;
cognitive resources that the provider uses so not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:04.460
just through constraint but through making them&nbsp;
more conscious of their incorrect bias decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:12.620
the ai then will have the potential to alter um&nbsp;
implicit bias and finally there's the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:18.780
to disrupt the transfer of racialized knowledge&nbsp;
through in informal cultural transmission that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:24.860
happens both at home through primary socialization&nbsp;
and through medical education so as medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:32.540
students and residents learn to rely on algorithms&nbsp;
a part of their cognitive toolkit the influence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:38.220
racialized views from their upbringing or as&nbsp;
dispensed from professors or attendings will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:46.460
wane but in order for this promise to come to pass&nbsp;
we do have to pay careful attention to the perils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:53.740
because it is i would argue an unfortunate truth&nbsp;
that the same mechanisms that have the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:59.580
to reduce provider bias may simply shift the&nbsp;
decisions to other institutional locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:05.100
where the source of the bias will be more hidden&nbsp;
and thus less unaccountable to critique and change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:14.700
so while ai promises to intervene on provider&nbsp;
bias it does so by decreasing autonomy so when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:20.940
decrease the scope and number of decisions that&nbsp;
workers make the sort of power embedded in those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:28.300
decisions doesn't disappear it simply moves so&nbsp;
one of the things that i hope to illustrate today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:34.060
is that the places where it moves to are&nbsp;
sometimes difficult to trace to hold to account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:38.140
and the decisions therefore more&nbsp;
difficult to intervene on and to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:46.540
and also although there's nothing particularly&nbsp;
laudable about provider bias right the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:52.220
thing it has going for it is that it's not&nbsp;
particularly efficient and what i mean by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:57.660
is that individual workers are biased in slightly&nbsp;
different ways which means that the impact then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:04.780
is variable so if we think to that 2016 study that&nbsp;
looked at racial the possession of racial beliefs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:11.180
in med students and residents not all students&nbsp;
held those beliefs right and holding those beliefs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:18.380
did not change the decision-making of everyone&nbsp;
who held them however when aspects of the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:24.620
are moved from this landscape of individual&nbsp;
variability you may get less bias but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:31.180
may also get more efficient bias bias with no&nbsp;
variability such that racial beliefs become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:38.140
in effect racial facts produced and reproduced&nbsp;
with 100 efficiency by the algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:45.020
and finally in my talk i want us to think about&nbsp;
and think through the conditions of the worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:51.660
because one of the things that workers do is&nbsp;
participate in everyday acts of resistance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:57.340
employer control right so when we think about&nbsp;
algorithmic logics they're not just shaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:03.420
some kind of abstract encounter but shaping the&nbsp;
behavior of employees through surveillance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:11.180
constraint so when you have a society that is&nbsp;
structured so unrepentantly around inequality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:18.620
right the sort of um spaces of resistance are&nbsp;
often the moments where workers make a way out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:25.500
no way and in healthcare they do this not just for&nbsp;
those for themselves but for their patients so i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:32.620
want to consider the healthcare worker as not just&nbsp;
a source of technical work or a dispenser of acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:38.620
or activities that can be made more efficient&nbsp;
and less biased but as a worker who improvises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:43.820
and through doing so creates the ground for&nbsp;
thinking about and organizing a new reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:50.380
so in the remainder of my talk i want to flesh&nbsp;
out some of these particular perils through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:57.900
looking at a series of illustrative cases so you&nbsp;
know even though we're all poised to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:02.140
ai as being something that exists in&nbsp;
the future i often find it helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:08.460
to begin in the past because often the things&nbsp;
that we think are new are actually quite old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:14.860
so i want to start with a case that probably many&nbsp;
of you are familiar with and that's the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:20.460
the spirometer and i want to start with this case&nbsp;
you know not because it's really an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:25.900
algorithmic thinking but it's our decision&nbsp;
making but because it's an example of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:32.380
efficient reproduction of the kind of racial facts&nbsp;
that often come to underlie such applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:37.900
so i'm taking this sort of historical&nbsp;
analysis and understanding of the spirometer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:43.420
from the work of historian union lonnie lundy&nbsp;
braun her book breathing race into the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:50.300
so this barometer the spirometer is a fairly&nbsp;
simple mechanical instrument used in pulmonary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:56.540
medicine it measures lung function it generates a&nbsp;
value that providers use to diagnose respiratory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:03.420
diseases but this tool the one that we use today&nbsp;
is an updated version of one that was developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:09.260
during the 1850s during the era of slavery&nbsp;
it was originally developed this barometer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:14.380
in written to measure the lung function of&nbsp;
industrial workers white industrial workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:19.820
but when it moved and technology moved across&nbsp;
the ocean and arrived in the u.s it was taken up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:27.660
by dr samuel cartwright he was a physician that&nbsp;
practiced in the antebellum south and he had a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:34.220
hand in taking this technology and developing for&nbsp;
use here so some of that development was technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:41.420
but really mostly it was rhetorical because dr&nbsp;
cartwright was a proponent of racial science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:46.940
he believed that the racists were different and&nbsp;
that black people in particular were biologically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:54.060
inferior to whites so for example he is very well&nbsp;
known for arguing before during and after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.060 --> 00:19:00.300
civil war that one measure of black inferiority&nbsp;
is that we supposedly had weaker lung functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:07.900
and he used this as an explanation of why&nbsp;
enforced labor either through forcing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:13.100
work of the enslaved or through the forced&nbsp;
agricultural labor that came after emancipation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:20.380
he used this to argue that this sort of forced&nbsp;
hard manual labor was not detrimental to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:25.900
health of black americans but was actually good&nbsp;
and beneficial for us therefore legitimating our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:31.980
place in the labor market in these places so one&nbsp;
of the things that i think is really important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:38.700
remember in talking about this piece of technology&nbsp;
is that the spirometer was not a clinical tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:46.140
cartwright did not use it to diagnose or&nbsp;
treat patients black nor white he used it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:52.780
to literally produce race a believer in racial&nbsp;
science he went looking for racial differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:59.260
and when you go looking for something you often&nbsp;
find it so one might even say that in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:04.860
to measuring lung function that one of the core&nbsp;
functions of the spirometer is that it was used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:10.620
to produce racial difference which for cartwright&nbsp;
was synonymous with producing a racial hierarchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:19.980
so in her expectation of the case um wendy brown's&nbsp;
work notes that there was disagreement around this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:26.300
sort of use of the spirometer and disagreement&nbsp;
around the sort of basic fact or argument about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:31.900
black americans having lower lung function right&nbsp;
and i think this is really important when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:37.340
talking about a historical case that people often&nbsp;
project back in history that everybody thought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:43.020
same way in a kind of backwards ideology but even&nbsp;
at the time there was contention and disagreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:48.940
you had other white physicians who had had the&nbsp;
occasion um to evaluate the fitness of black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:55.420
